NAME
       git - track changes to files

SYNOPSIS
       git <command> [arguments]

DESCRIPTION
       Keeps the history of a directory the way git does, in the same
       object format, so a repository made here can be read, checked
       and cloned by git on another machine.

COMMANDS
       init [directory]
              Start a repository. Without an argument it uses the
              current directory.

       clone <url> [directory]
              Copy a repository from an HTTP server. It asks what
              branches the server has, takes the one called main or
              master, fetches a pack of everything reachable, unpacks
              it - deltas included - and writes the files out.

              Only http:// and https:// are understood; the ssh and
              git protocols are not. The clone is complete history,
              not a shallow copy.

       add <file>...
              Stage files for the next commit. Each file is stored as
              an object straight away, so staging is what fixes the
              content, not committing.

       rm <file>...
              Take files out of the staging area. The files themselves
              are left alone.

       status
              Which branch this is, what is staged, and whether a
              staged file has changed since it was staged.

       commit -m <message>
              Record everything staged. The author comes from
              GIT_AUTHOR, or is 'Cervus user <user@cervus>'.

       log    The commit leading here and every commit before it.

       push [url] [branch]
              Send commits to a server. Without arguments it uses the
              url recorded by clone or by remote add, and the branch
              HEAD is on.

              It asks the server which commit the branch is at, works
              out which objects the server is missing, builds a pack
              of them and posts it. Objects go in whole, without delta
              compression, so a push is larger on the wire than git's
              own would be but is read by any git.

              A server that wants credentials gets them from the url
              as https://user:token@host/path, or asks for them at the
              terminal. Nothing is written to disk: the url recorded
              for next time has the credentials removed.

       remote [-v | add <url> | remove]
              The server clone and push use. It is one line in
              .git/remote, not git's config format.

       show <object>, cat-file <hash>
              Print an object. A tree prints as one line per entry.

       hash-object [-w] <file>
              The name git would give the file. With -w it is also
              written into the repository.

       ls-files
              What is staged, with mode and hash.

OBJECTS
       Objects live in .git/objects, named by the SHA-1 of their
       contents with a header, and stored zlib-compressed - the same
       as git. Refs are files under .git/refs holding a hash, and
       .git/HEAD names the branch in use.

       A pack received from a server is unpacked into loose objects
       rather than kept as a pack, so a large repository takes more
       room here than it would elsewhere.

       The staging area is .git/index.txt, one line per file. Git's own
       index is a binary format this does not write, so git elsewhere
       will not see the staging area, only the commits.

       Trees nest the way git's do: a directory is its own tree object
       rather than a slash in a name, so git fsck --strict is happy
       with what is pushed. Cloning fills the staging area from the
       checkout, so the first commit after a clone keeps the files
       that were already there.

ENVIRONMENT
       GIT_AUTHOR
              Name and address for commits, as 'Name <address>'.

EXAMPLE
         git init
         echo 'hello' > readme.txt
         git add readme.txt
         git commit -m 'first commit'
         git log
         git push https://user:token@example.com/repo.git

